---
import BriefMethodologyTemplate, {
  type MethodologySection,
} from "../../../templates/BriefMethodologyTemplate.astro";
import { DISCOVERY_CALL_URL } from "../../../config/cta";

const discoveryCall = DISCOVERY_CALL_URL.en;
const whitepaperUrl = "https://tally.so/r/xX0VYv";

// Inline-style fragment for citation code samples. v24 cream page so the
// code block uses cream-deep bg rather than gray-50.
const codeBlockStyle =
  "background: #F2EEDF; padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px; " +
  "font-family: 'Space Mono', ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace; " +
  "font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.7; overflow-x: auto; white-space: pre; " +
  "margin: 0 0 16px; color: #1a1a1a;";

const sections: MethodologySection[] = [
  {
    heading: "What Lemma Critical Brief is",
    body: `<p>Lemma Critical Brief analyses real cyber attacks and AI and record failures, one incident at a time, from primary sources. Each Brief sets out what got through as a legitimate operation, and what could be checked before execution to tell it apart — in a form any organisation can apply regardless of the attack route. It is written for executives, IT and operations staff who need to check and explain their own position.</p>
<p>It covers attack incidents (impersonation, account takeover, tampering, abuse of authority) and AI-era trust failures (inputs to AI decisions, integrity of records, agent authority). Both happen where nothing checks, apart from appearance, whether what arrived or what was recorded is genuine.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Detection and the pre-execution check",
    body: `<p>Most organisations defend with <strong>detection</strong>: noticing intrusions and anomalies after they happen. Detection is essential. But operations that look genuine — a valid login, a valid signature, a page served by the real site — can go through before anyone notices.</p>
<p>Lemma's approach sits in front of that: a <strong>pre-execution check</strong>. Before a payment, withdrawal or publication runs, it checks that it came from an issuer with authority, stays within the delegated scope, and has not been altered. It complements detection rather than replacing it.</p>
<p>Briefs never treat what was not disclosed as what was missing. Instead of saying what a company lacked, they set out what any organisation in the same position can check, whatever the route. They never say "Lemma would have prevented this".</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Brief structure",
    body: `<p>From November 2026, Briefs use six chapters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>01 Summary</strong> — what happened and what holds regardless of the route, in one paragraph</li>
<li><strong>02 What got through</strong> — the operation that went through as legitimate, what reached the receiving side, and what was not disclosed</li>
<li><strong>03 What to check</strong> — who issues the approval, where it is checked, and what record remains, with an exhibit on detection vs the pre-execution check</li>
<li><strong>04 What Lemma does</strong> — current controls compared with Lemma in a table split by operation type, including where Lemma does not help, plus a checklist</li>
<li><strong>05 Facts and timeline</strong> — disclosed facts, timeline and the response after disclosure</li>
<li><strong>06 Sources</strong> — primary sources first, each labelled with its standing (primary, reporting, etc.)</li>
</ul>
<p>Earlier Briefs keep the previous order, which starts from the incident (TL;DR / What happened / Timeline / Why it wasn't stopped / How to counter this / Sources).</p>
<p>The voice is plain. Detection products and existing vendors are not framed as adversaries but as complementary roles. Links to related Briefs are generated from each Brief's classification.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Pillar / Category framework",
    body: `<p>Briefs are classified according to Lemma's <strong>4 Pillars</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pillar 01 — Verifiable Origin</strong>: the layer that independently verifies the origin of messages, data, and code</li>
<li><strong>Pillar 02 — Verifiable AI</strong>: the layer that ZK-commits the process of AI judgment</li>
<li><strong>Pillar 03 — Agent Authority Proof</strong>: the layer that records and proves the delegation relationships of agents</li>
<li><strong>Pillar 04 — Regulatory Attribute Proof</strong>: the layer that proves KYC / AML / regulatory attributes via selective disclosure</li>
</ul>
<p>Each Brief carries a <code>primary_category</code> (main category) and <code>secondary_categories</code> (cross-cutting categories), making it accessible from both the Pillar archive and the Category archive.</p>
<p>Category names are defined independently by Lemma. Crossover with external classifications (AIID, OECD AIID, etc.) is expressed through <code>secondary_categories</code>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Citation conventions",
    body: `<p>Each Brief has a Permanent URL (the slug never changes). The number of a withdrawn Brief may be reused by the next Brief published, so cite the URL, not the number alone.</p>
<p>Citation is recommended in the following formats. <strong>Plain text</strong> uses Brief 001 as a concrete example; <strong>BibTeX</strong> and <strong>APA</strong> are shown in template form.</p>
<p><strong>Plain text</strong></p>
<pre style="${codeBlockStyle}"><code>Lemma Critical Team. (2026).
"KelpDAO / rsETH Unauthorized Unlock — RPC Manipulation Attack on the DVN Observation Layer".
Lemma Critical Brief No.001. Lemma / FRAME00, Inc.
https://lemma.frame00.com/critical/briefs/001-kelpdao-rseth/</code></pre>
<p><strong>BibTeX</strong></p>
<pre style="${codeBlockStyle}"><code>@techreport{lemma_critical_&lt;NN&gt;,
  author      = {{Lemma Critical Team}},
  title       = {&lt;Title&gt;},
  number      = {&lt;NN&gt;},
  year        = {2026},
  institution = {Lemma / FRAME00, Inc.},
  url         = {&lt;Brief URL&gt;}
}</code></pre>
<p><strong>APA</strong></p>
<pre style="${codeBlockStyle}"><code>Lemma Critical Team. (2026).
&lt;Title&gt; (Lemma Critical Brief No.&lt;Number&gt;). Lemma / FRAME00, Inc.
&lt;Brief URL&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Each Brief page provides a "Cite this Brief" box with the three formats selectable via tabs.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Updates and corrections policy",
    body: `<p>After publication, each Brief is maintained according to the following policy:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Typos / minor corrections</strong> (misspellings, broken links, notation inconsistencies): overwritten in place, no version change</li>
<li><strong>Content revisions</strong> (factual additions, interpretive changes, incorporation of new facts): retained via version numbering. The frontmatter <code>version</code> increments from 1.0 → 1.1 (minor) or 2.0 (major). The template renders a "Revision History" (current version) at the foot of the Brief. Where a summary of the changes is needed, a revision blockquote sits at the top of the body</li>
<li><strong>Retraction</strong> (when misinformation or misclassification is identified): a retraction notice is left at the original URL and the content is withdrawn. Per the Permanent URL policy, the URL itself is not deleted</li>
</ul>`,
  },
  {
    heading: "Distribution and contact",
    body: `<p>Lemma Critical Brief is a structured analysis of public information. The series is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Public analysis, not an audit, assessment, or recommendation for any specific organization</strong></li>
<li>For use in decision-making, direct consultation with your organization's Lemma Critical contact is recommended</li>
<li>For commercial use or translation inquiries, please contact the Lemma editorial team</li>
</ul>
<p>Contact:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Discovery Call</strong>: <a href="${discoveryCall}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${discoveryCall.replace("https://", "")}</a> — for tips, corrections, or commercial-use inquiries</li>
<li><strong>Whitepaper request</strong>: <a href="${whitepaperUrl}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${whitepaperUrl.replace("https://", "")}</a></li>
</ul>`,
  },
];

const description =
  "Methodology, thesis, and citation conventions of the Lemma Critical Brief series. The production and publication of each Brief, as well as citations by readers, follow the principles on this page.";
---

<BriefMethodologyTemplate
  locale="en"
  tag="Lemma Critical Brief"
  title="Methodology"
  lastUpdated="Last updated: 2026-08-28"
  intro="This page documents the methodology, thesis, and citation conventions for the Lemma Critical Brief series. The production and publication of each Brief, as well as citations by readers, follow the principles below."
  sections={sections}
  description={description}
/>
